msgid "Disk troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:7
#: 50f513f5f1ff4594a7631463d4a6ee8f
msgid "\"Out of disk space\" error"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:10
#: 73a86fab97b04956a924699eea9bbbad
msgid ""
"If the disk is completely full, you will get an ``Out of disk space`` error "
"that may crash your system because Dom0 does not have enough disk space to "
"work. So it’s good practice to regularly check disk space usage. Running the "
"``df -h`` command in dom0 terminal will show some information, but not "
"include all the relevant details. The Qubes user interface provides a :"
"guilabel:`Qubes Disk Space widget`. If you are unable to access the "
"interface, the command line version is running ``sudo lvs | head`` and "
"looking at top entry for LVM pool. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:21
#: 9a64d845d2e640a3a4834b6be4819750
msgid ""
"If you run ``df -h``, it only shows the information in the ``root`` line "
"(which is already included in the ``pool00`` line). As you can see, the "
"``sudo lvs | head`` command includes additional important columns ``Data%`` "
"and ``Meta%``, shown in the above example to have the values 89% and 69% "
"respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:23
#: 6a57b925c4b84ac8b92dc6e76a01f5d8
msgid ""
"If your system is able to boot, but cannot load a desktop environment, it is "
"possible to login to dom0 terminal with Alt + Ctrl + F2."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:25
#: 0325be93202a4ef6bd2eba211fb5c6a8
msgid ""
"If this does not work, check the size of ``/var/lib/qubes/qubes.xml``. If it "
"is zero, you’ll need to use one of the file backup (stored in ``/var/lib/"
"qubes/backup``), hopefully you have the current data there. Find the most "
"recent one and place in ``/var/lib/qubes/qubes.xml`` instead of the empty "
"file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:27
#: f5a3f5620acf4ca598562e4a7c521cdd
msgid ""
"In any case you’ll need some disk space to start the VM. Check ``df -h`` "
"output if you have some. If not, here are some hints how to free some disk "
"space:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:29
#: f3728ef5f861487ca6f05c4a66445a85
msgid "Clean yum cache."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:37
#: 24d74d3794e149ffb02ed4df08194c64
msgid ""
"Delete ``.img`` files of a less important VM, which can be found in ``/var/"
"lib/qubes/appvms/``. Then, when the system is working again, clean up the "
"rest."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:44
#: d666e5c0dc214cb0956448a0e5bd8e99
msgid ""
"With this method, you lose the data of one VM, but it’ll work more reliably."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:46
#: 8d7712b79dc740e29ca8cc6193400c50
msgid "Decrease the filesystem safety margin (5% by default)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:54
#: 7897c6aac29043898aba22df504916ab
msgid ""
"Remove some unneeded files in dom0 home (if you have any, most likely not). "
"Also look for unneeded files in ``/var/log`` in dom0, and ``/var/log/qubes``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:58
#: 709d7750de9941ce8dcb51703bb48868
msgid ""
"The above steps applies to old VM disks format. These steps may work on "
"Qubes 4.0, but are not default anymore. By default, Qubes 4.0 now uses LVM. "
"The equivalent steps are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:60
#: e6da3938a5bc4db8878eca351654cf10
msgid "Get a list of VM disks using ``sudo lvs``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:62
#: 59e1ef509b5244c1a7cfea664e392506
msgid ""
"Use ``sudo lvremove qubes_dom0/<name>`` to remove backup copies of some less "
"important VMs – entries with ``-back`` in their name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:64
#: c460447f954746f48b142ddb04b54638
msgid ""
"If that isn’t enough, remove actual disks of less important VMs. NOTE: You "
"will lose the data of that VM, but your system will resume working."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:68
#: 8d1ceba7f751488195c73e4eb01be895
msgid "For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:97
#: 91816078a9444012a5d8f86f0e7fc7da
msgid ""
"After freeing some initial space, it may be possible to recover more space "
"by deleting files in a userVM after connecting to the userVM terminal:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:106
#: f23734979b4a456396d040a7fa1f0178
msgid ""
"Since ``qvm-console-dispvm`` requires working graphical user interface "
"login, you must first free enough space to be able to start a VM and login "
"to graphical UI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:109
#: dc4adcc6c13d45d1a1334681c9f70141
msgid "Can't resize VM storage / \"resize2fs: Permission denied\" error"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:112
#: 93b0a1b12ac14497b4f01848b4e90f45
msgid ""
":doc:`Resizing a volume </user/advanced-topics/resize-disk-image>` in the "
"Qubes interface should be a straightforward process. But sometimes, an "
"attempt to resize will look like it worked, when it in fact fails silently. "
"If you then try the same operation in the dom0 console using the ``qvm-"
"volume extend`` command, it fails with the error message: ``resize2fs: "
"Permission denied to resize filesystem``. This error indicates that a "
"``resize2fs`` will not work, unless ``fsck`` is run first. Qubes OS "
"utilities cannot yet handle this case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/disk-troubleshooting.rst:114
#: 264f6bd8a2ea42bbbeedf03ddcfd408b
